501
00:38:48,480 --> 00:38:59,360
[SPEAKER_02] If you don't have a repeatable value prop that can sustain long term retention and your primary growth loop is paid ads, that's where you see a graveyard of companies that have just completely failed.

502
00:38:59,520 --> 00:39:03,040
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah, because you eventually run out out, your cat goes up as you expend your users.

503
00:39:03,200 --> 00:39:10,160
[SPEAKER_00] And actually, I think that's kind of the, just to jump to the next R, this is remarkable and like efficiency and virality, right?

504
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:11,840
[SPEAKER_00] It's part of that capture.

505
00:39:12,080 --> 00:39:13,360
[SPEAKER_00] So what do you mean by remarkable?

506
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:14,800
[SPEAKER_00] Sorry, David, I stole your job.

507
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:15,760
[SPEAKER_00] It's fun.

508
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:16,480
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

509
00:39:16,800 --> 00:39:26,880
[SPEAKER_02] So remarkable is the idea that there are stand out companies like Tender, Strava that have built these explosive experiences, right?

510
00:39:27,040 --> 00:39:30,960
[SPEAKER_02] And with Tender, it was the swipe mechanic, among other things with Strava.

511
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:33,840
[SPEAKER_02] It was this highly engaged community of athletes.

512
00:39:34,000 --> 00:39:35,840
[SPEAKER_02] If it didn't happen on Strava, it didn't happen.

513
00:39:36,160 --> 00:39:37,360
[SPEAKER_02] But those are the exceptions.

514
00:39:37,680 --> 00:39:50,400
[SPEAKER_02] Even if you're not a product that depends primarily on virality in order to grow your user base, ideally you want a product that is unique and compelling enough that people want to talk about it.

515
00:39:50,880 --> 00:39:56,240
[SPEAKER_02] And you may not have a K factor over one, but people are going to tell their friends and family members about it.

516
00:39:56,400 --> 00:39:57,760
[SPEAKER_02] And that does two things that are important.

517
00:39:58,400 --> 00:40:03,120
[SPEAKER_02] One, it helps drive more organic acquisition, which brings down blended CAC and gives you a more sustainable business model.

518
00:40:03,360 --> 00:40:09,600
[SPEAKER_02] But the other thing it does is it creates this installed community of users who are really loyal and talking to each other.

519
00:40:10,000 --> 00:40:16,400
[SPEAKER_02] And again, Strava is like a stand out example of this., but when you have that, it also really helps with long term retention.

520
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:23,760
[SPEAKER_02] And that's equally, if not more important at the end of the day than, you know, having a viral product that has a K factor over the one, because that's really, really hard to come by.

521
00:40:24,320 --> 00:40:29,760
[SPEAKER_01] I think this is something people assume, Oh, if I just build a great product, people will talk about it or whatever.

522
00:40:29,840 --> 00:40:33,680
[SPEAKER_01] And to some extent, that's true, but the remarkable part is really interesting.

523
00:40:34,000 --> 00:41:00,080
[SPEAKER_01] And again, just being fresh on my mind, having talked to Ryan recently, the most remarkable part about Flighty and the stories that get told is when Flighty tells people six hours in advance that their flight is going to be delayed or two hours in advance and they can actually see that the plane that's supposed to take them wherever they're going hasn't even landed at the airport or whatever and they get to spend three more hours at home with their family instead of sitting at the airport for three hours.

524
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:02,800
[SPEAKER_01] That's what people remark when they talk about Flighty.

525
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:06,480
[SPEAKER_01] It's like, yeah, it's a great flight tracker and yes, it has all these other great features.

526
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:12,720
[SPEAKER_01] It's probably a good exercise for founders and growth teams to think like, what is remarkable about our product?

527
00:41:12,960 --> 00:41:15,680
[SPEAKER_01] And in that word, it's like, what will people actually say?

528
00:41:15,920 --> 00:41:18,800
[SPEAKER_01] What are people actually saying about our product when they share?

529
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:24,160
[SPEAKER_01] Is there something that people care enough about that they're actually going to talk about it?

530
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:26,720
[SPEAKER_01] And can we make it easy, right?

531
00:41:27,040 --> 00:41:27,200
[SPEAKER_00] Right.

532
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:27,520
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

533
00:41:28,000 --> 00:41:29,360
[SPEAKER_00] Do something to facilitate that.

534
00:41:29,760 --> 00:41:29,840
[UNKNOWN] Yeah.

535
00:41:30,080 --> 00:41:36,720
[SPEAKER_02] Well, and two other things that I think a lot of companies don't think about when it comes to having a remarkable product that drives word of mouth.

536
00:41:36,800 --> 00:41:40,480
[SPEAKER_02] So one is there are different types of word of mouth loops.

537
00:41:40,640 --> 00:41:46,560
[SPEAKER_02] There's the like Facebook example of it just went viral like wildfire at Harvard and then everywhere else.

538
00:41:47,200 --> 00:41:48,880
[SPEAKER_02] But that's not the only type of virality.

539
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:53,600
[SPEAKER_02] So you have a personal viral loop, which is people are inviting people to the product because it makes the product more valuable to them.

540
00:41:53,920 --> 00:42:00,960
[SPEAKER_02] You have a social viral loop, which is where people are telling other people about the product because they want to be one of the cool kids who knew about it early on and sort of spread the word.

541
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:08,720
[SPEAKER_02] You have a financial viral loop, which is you're actually getting a monetary or non monetary reward for inviting other people.

542
00:42:08,880 --> 00:42:14,720
[SPEAKER_02] And so there are subscription apps like Masterclass, for example, has done a really good job with that type of growth strategy.

543
00:42:15,200 --> 00:42:23,120
[SPEAKER_02] And then there are casual contact viral loops where people at a party see someone swiping on Tinder and they're like, oh, that's interesting.

544
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:24,560
[SPEAKER_02] Maybe I should download Tinder.

545
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:32,320
[SPEAKER_02] Or people are at a marathon and they see people recording their run on Strava and they think, oh, maybe I should record my run on Strava.

546
00:42:32,560 --> 00:42:41,680
[SPEAKER_02] So you have to think about what your product is naturally suited for when it comes to how it's going to drive word of mouth and how you design it to match that flavor of word of mouth.

547
00:42:42,160 --> 00:42:50,800
[SPEAKER_02] And then I think the other thing that people miss is the fact that so much word of mouth happens offline and you can't measure it easily.

548
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:59,920
[SPEAKER_02] And so there are exceptions where you can just track directly in the app, like someone sent a referral code or they shared an invitation through this channel and there's a unique parameter.

549
00:43:00,080 --> 00:43:04,720
[SPEAKER_02] And so we know how many people invited, how many friends they invited and how many of those friends converted.

550
00:43:05,200 --> 00:43:06,880
[SPEAKER_02] But most of the activity is happening offline.

551
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:12,880
[SPEAKER_02] And so there's a tool called the word of mouth coefficient that allows you to quantify offline word of mouth.

552
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:20,160
[SPEAKER_02] by looking at how many new users are you getting through channels like direct branded search or social in any given period.

553
00:43:20,400 --> 00:43:26,560
[SPEAKER_02] So look at it over the course of a week, a month over your returning users plus any new users getting through nonorganic channels.

554
00:43:27,040 --> 00:43:33,360
[SPEAKER_02] And that coefficient is telling you how efficiently you're getting new users through word of mouth from your existing user base.

555
00:43:33,680 --> 00:43:42,000
[SPEAKER_02] And so that's nice because it goes beyond tools like NPS that aren't really telling you anything actionable about what you can do to a tool that quantifies it in more detail.

556
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:56,000
[SPEAKER_00] The word of mouth coefficient I found I googled it because I need to google things, but there's an ar a really interesting concept of using basically subtracting all your knowns and then it's like assuming that that's the remainder is your word of mouth.

557
00:43:56,080 --> 00:43:57,120
[SPEAKER_00] Is that like the basic concept?

558
00:43:57,440 --> 00:43:58,240
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, that's the idea.

559
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:10,800
[SPEAKER_02] You're basically backing into what we can directionally estimate we're getting in terms of word of mouth new user acquisition through returning users and new users who are not coming in through organic channels.

560
00:44:10,960 --> 00:44:12,560
[SPEAKER_02] So that could be paid ads.

561
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:15,120
[SPEAKER_02] It could be non branded search traffic.

562
00:44:15,360 --> 00:44:26,240
[SPEAKER_02] And the reason it's powerful is it helps you understand one like overall similar to K factor, roughly how many new word of mouth users are we gettinging for an existing active user?

563
00:44:26,880 --> 00:44:34,560
[SPEAKER_02] The second reason it's important is because depending on the nature of your product, you can actually see word-to-mouth coefficient fluctuate quite a bit for various reasons.

564
00:44:34,800 --> 00:44:50,720
[SPEAKER_02] And so I spent time at Quizlet looking at this, but I've also worked with several ed-tech companies as part of my advising business, and one of the things you often see in ed-tech is your word-to-mouth coefficient goes way down over the summer and during holidays, which makes sense because nobody is in school.

565
00:44:51,360 --> 00:45:00,160
[SPEAKER_02] Then there are these critical back to school periods around August, September in North America as well as after the holidays where your word-to-mouth coefficient spikes way up.

566
00:45:00,640 --> 00:45:09,280
[SPEAKER_02] And then there are also a few periods in the middle of the school year around exams where you have these windows of opportunity to really take advantage of a much higher word of mouth coefficient.

567
00:45:09,440 --> 00:45:19,280
[SPEAKER_02] And then the last piece that was even more interesting was during the pandemic, you saw word of mouth coefficient fall off a cliff when the pandemic first hit for a lot of ed tech companies.

568
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:23,120
[SPEAKER_02] But then what happened after that really varied by country.

569
00:45:24,000 --> 00:45:28,640
[SPEAKER_02] And so you had countries that kicked into gear with remote learning really quickly.

570
00:45:29,280 --> 00:45:34,480
[SPEAKER_02] And in those countries, word of mouth coefficients actually ended up higher than they were before the pandemic in some cases.

571
00:45:34,960 --> 00:45:39,840
[SPEAKER_02] And then you had other countries that took much longer to recover, and you could see all this in the word of mouth data.

572
00:45:40,240 --> 00:45:47,360
[SPEAKER_02] And so it's a pretty actionable metric in terms of identifying when you have these pockets of opportunity to capture more virality.

573
00:45:47,760 --> 00:45:53,920
[SPEAKER_02] And then when you pair that with the type of virality that your product drives, you can think more tactically around, okay, what does that mean in terms of what do we do in the product?

574
00:45:54,160 --> 00:46:00,640
[SPEAKER_01] Well, I think that's a great place to wrap up the four Rs of value creation, robust, rapid, repeatable, and remarkable.

575
00:46:01,680 --> 00:46:03,600
[SPEAKER_01] The alliteration is amusing.

576
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:08,160
[SPEAKER_01] I love your consultant background and alliterating these, but it is actually helpful.

577
00:46:08,320 --> 00:46:11,600
[SPEAKER_01] Like, I'm going to remember that robust, rapid, repeatable, remarkable.

578
00:46:12,000 --> 00:46:12,480
[SPEAKER_02] That's the idea.

579
00:46:12,880 --> 00:46:14,960
[SPEAKER_02] If someone doesn't remember it, then it never happened.

580
00:46:15,920 --> 00:46:16,240
[SPEAKER_01] All right.

581
00:46:16,400 --> 00:46:18,400
[SPEAKER_01] So let's move on to value delivery.

582
00:46:18,640 --> 00:46:24,080
[SPEAKER_01] What do you mean by value delivery in the context of this subscription value loop?

583
00:46:24,400 --> 00:46:24,480
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah.

584
00:46:24,720 --> 00:46:36,000
[SPEAKER_02] So value delivery is the idea of, okay, you have a product that people care about and want to use, how do you, as cost efficiently as possible, get it into the hands of as many users as possible and connect them to that value?

585
00:46:36,320 --> 00:46:55,840
[SPEAKER_02] And the reason this is so important for consumer subscription apps, especially now, is because the playbook for much of the last decade has been find a product that people are interested in and then go spend a bunch of money on Google and Meta to acquire users and make sure LTVCAC is high enough for the business to work and keep your fingers crossed that that will last.

586
00:46:56,160 --> 00:47:01,120
[SPEAKER_02] I'm oversimplifying it, but that playbook no longer works for a variety of reasons.

587
00:47:01,760 --> 00:47:04,960
[SPEAKER_02] One, the app stores have just become much more crowded, so there's a lot more competition.

588
00:47:05,680 --> 00:47:12,880
[SPEAKER_02] Two, there's all of the challenges presented by ATT and iOS 14 where paid marketing is a lot less efficient than it used to be.

589
00:47:13,200 --> 00:47:18,720
[SPEAKER_02] And then the third, which is not the most important, but it's a factor is venture capital funding is harder to come by now.

590
00:47:19,040 --> 00:47:33,120
[SPEAKER_02] And so if your plan was, well, we're going to scale until we hit a tipping point where the economics work and we can do that because we're going to raise a large seed in Series A round that doesn't work anymore, at least not in this market environment.

591
00:47:33,360 --> 00:47:34,800
[SPEAKER_01] Certainly not in consumer, right?

592
00:47:35,600 --> 00:47:44,480
[SPEAKER_01] And not when interest rates are non-zero, that capital needs a faster return on investment and a more sure return on investment.

593
00:47:44,960 --> 00:47:45,040
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah.

594
00:47:45,200 --> 00:47:51,120
[SPEAKER_02] So I was going to say, I'm not going to go into as much detail on value delivery as I do on value creation and value capture.

595
00:47:51,440 --> 00:48:00,480
[SPEAKER_02] But the number one point I want to make on value delivery is that the best consumer subscription companies, almost without exception, and those that have really stood the test of time.

596
00:48:00,480 --> 00:48:08,640
[SPEAKER_02] And they've been able to pass the test of time, have really robust organic acquisition strategies that are their primary growth lever, and they only use paid ads as a supplement.

597
00:48:09,040 --> 00:48:10,160
[SPEAKER_01] What are some examples of that?

598
00:48:10,640 --> 00:48:12,800
[SPEAKER_02] So I think you can divide them into two categories.

599
00:48:13,120 --> 00:48:16,800
[SPEAKER_02] You have companies that have really been able to grow through word of mouth.

600
00:48:16,960 --> 00:48:18,080
[SPEAKER_02] The other big one is content.

601
00:48:18,960 --> 00:48:20,640
[SPEAKER_02] So there are a few different flavors of this.

602
00:48:20,880 --> 00:48:26,960
[SPEAKER_02] Quizlet is an example of a company that has done a really nice job growing through long tail SEO, right?

603
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:30,880
[SPEAKER_02] There's so much flashcards content created on Quizlet by students, by teachers.

604
00:48:31,440 --> 00:48:40,640
[SPEAKER_02] And so even though there are plenty of other ed tech companies out there competing for head terms, Quizlet has a really strong advantage in long tail search queries.

605
00:48:41,120 --> 00:48:48,400
[SPEAKER_02] So that's one good example of a company that has been able to find this kind of unique way of scaling without having to spend a lot of money.

606
00:48:48,880 --> 00:48:51,440
[SPEAKER_02] Another good example is AllTrails, which I know has been on the podcast.

607
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:57,520
[SPEAKER_02] And for them, long tail search, I'm sure, was relevant as well, but hyper local search, I'm sure, was a big advantage for them.

608
00:48:57,680 --> 00:49:06,480
[SPEAKER_02] Because if I'm looking for where I want to go on a hike or a trail run, I'm probably, by and large, looking within 5, 10 miles of my home.

609
00:49:07,200 --> 00:49:24,400
[SPEAKER_02] And so, as is the case with all these things, you sort of have to double click and triple click before you get to the real insights., but I think companies that can generate user generated content that's organic to the product experience and ideally pair that user generated content with a unique and differentiated SEO strategy can really be successful over the long run.

610
00:49:24,640 --> 00:49:28,320
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, and there's probably like ten other ways that that works.

611
00:49:28,800 --> 00:49:38,800
[SPEAKER_01] We could probably do a whole podcast on all the different kinds of organic loops that work for different apps for different reasons, for different contexts, for different markets, for different whatever.

612
00:49:39,200 --> 00:49:40,640
[SPEAKER_01] But yeah, I think that's a good summary.

613
00:49:40,800 --> 00:49:48,320
[SPEAKER_01] We do talk a ton on the podcast about value delivery, about getting attention for your app, about getting people to the app store, about marketing and everything else.

614
00:49:48,960 --> 00:50:01,200
[SPEAKER_00] One point that Phil has in the notes here, and we talked about earlier, there's always this wall eventually that LTV and CAC kind of normalize because you push beyond your product market fit, you've pushed into a less spendy market.

615
00:50:01,520 --> 00:50:02,960
[SPEAKER_00] Like eventually it loses steam.

616
00:50:03,120 --> 00:50:09,280
[SPEAKER_00] It might be at scale A, scale B, scale C, right, you know, some order of magnitude difference between those two, but it will die out.

617
00:50:09,600 --> 00:50:13,520
[SPEAKER_00] All the things we talked about before make that number a little bit further in the future.

618
00:50:13,920 --> 00:50:20,160
[SPEAKER_00] Better word of mouth, all these things, like better product, make that all a little bit more efficient, but at the end of the day, you're only going to be able to scale it so far.

619
00:50:20,560 --> 00:50:24,320
[SPEAKER_00] And so, I mean, I think that just supports your point, and which is a benefit for the world.

620
00:50:24,800 --> 00:50:26,640
[SPEAKER_00] Like, I think we've entered an even better era.

621
00:50:27,040 --> 00:50:30,240
[SPEAKER_00] I wasn't super excited with the Zurp era of apps, as we mentioned before.

622
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:33,920
[SPEAKER_00] And I think we've entered a better world where, as you said, talking about value capture, right?

623
00:50:34,080 --> 00:50:36,560
[SPEAKER_00] Like value creation, value capture, delivery and all that stuff.

624
00:50:36,720 --> 00:50:37,920
[SPEAKER_00] Let's let the app store handle that.

625
00:50:38,080 --> 00:50:38,880
[SPEAKER_00] That's why we've been there.

626
00:50:39,200 --> 00:50:40,640
[SPEAKER_00] You know, kind of an in-grade thing.

627
00:50:40,960 --> 00:50:43,440
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, there's this idea of painkillers versus vitamins.

628
00:50:43,680 --> 00:50:47,040
[SPEAKER_02] And I think Zurp allowed a lot of vitamins to hang around.

629
00:50:47,920 --> 00:50:54,400
[SPEAKER_02] But at the end of the day, if your product isn't solving a real need, then it probably won't last.

630
00:50:54,720 --> 00:51:01,280
[SPEAKER_02] And so I think as Zurp has subsided, there's just been a lot of companies left holding the bag who don't really have an ans answer for it.

631
00:51:01,840 --> 00:51:06,320
[SPEAKER_01] So the last part of the subscription value loop is value capture.

632
00:51:06,560 --> 00:51:08,960
[SPEAKER_01] And this is one I do want to deep dive into.

633
00:51:09,280 --> 00:51:14,320
[SPEAKER_01] And we do talk about this a lot on the podcast, but it's just such a fundamental part that's hard.

634
00:51:14,560 --> 00:51:25,600
[SPEAKER_01] You know, you create value, but capturing the value and finding the right balance of how much value you give to the users and how much value you take and finding that right balance is really tricky.

635
00:51:25,920 --> 00:51:28,080
[SPEAKER_01] So tell me about value capture.

636
00:51:28,640 --> 00:51:28,720
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah.

637
00:51:28,880 --> 00:51:33,840
[SPEAKER_02] So value capture is this idea that, to go back to the car analogy, your subscription revenue is your fuel.

638
00:51:34,160 --> 00:51:35,920
[SPEAKER_02] And so even if you build a really nice car.

639
00:51:36,800 --> 00:51:40,640
[SPEAKER_02] If it doesn't have enough fuel to run, it's ultimately going to sputter out and fail.

640
00:51:41,600 --> 00:51:52,320
[SPEAKER_02] And so I think what's interesting about this step is you can almost think of different categories of companies in terms of where they are strong versus weak in the subscription value loop.

641
00:51:52,480 --> 00:51:56,320
[SPEAKER_02] And so the companies that are really, really strong at value creation, they have a great product.

642
00:51:57,040 --> 00:51:59,280
[SPEAKER_02] They're going to get past that initial product market fit phase.

643
00:51:59,440 --> 00:52:08,000
[SPEAKER_02] They're at least going to get to the point where they're scaling the business, but they may run into a wall if they can't be efficient about one or both of value delivery and value capture.

644
00:52:08,400 --> 00:52:16,240
[SPEAKER_02] On the other end of the spectrum, you have the companies that are really good at value capture, but they're not good at val value capture, well, they're probably never really going to get much beyond the starting day, right?

645
00:52:16,400 --> 00:52:30,640
[SPEAKER_02] And so what's interesting about value capture, and a lot of my advising work centers around this step is you really want these companies to succeed that have done a great job building a great product, but they just haven't quite figured out how to get enough value back out of the system to keep the car running.

646
00:52:31,520 --> 00:52:40,080
[SPEAKER_02] And I even run sometimes into, we ran into this a little bit at Quizlet, where Quizlet is such a mission driven company and it's done so much good in the world for teachers and students.

647
00:52:40,400 --> 00:52:43,680
[SPEAKER_02] But there were times I was like, well, should we be monetizing this product less than we are?

648
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:45,840
[SPEAKER_02] Like, ultimately, this is an education app.

649
00:52:46,000 --> 00:52:47,680
[SPEAKER_02] We want to do everything we can to advance education.

650
00:52:48,400 --> 00:52:50,160
[SPEAKER_02] But it doesn't have to be a zero sum game.

651
00:53:10,080 --> 00:53:12,640
[SPEAKER_02] And so, value capture is all about making sure the car keeps running.

652
00:53:13,040 --> 00:53:14,160
[SPEAKER_00] You want to leave surplus, right?

653
00:53:14,320 --> 00:53:15,120
[SPEAKER_00] This is what you're saying.

654
00:53:15,280 --> 00:53:16,800
[SPEAKER_00] This is the debate on how much to capture.

655
00:53:17,200 --> 00:53:18,800
[SPEAKER_00] You can capture none, you'll go out of business.

656
00:53:19,360 --> 00:53:30,720
[SPEAKER_00] But, at the end of the day, you should be allowed to capture enough that you're still delivering a net value, you know, if you put dollars on it, you create $100 of value for your customer, you charge them 50, like they're still ahead.

657
00:53:31,040 --> 00:53:32,400
[SPEAKER_00] Nobody should be angry about that, right?

658
00:53:32,560 --> 00:53:39,440
[SPEAKER_00] Like people get really icky about monetization, but like with the energy analogies, again, I know, but it's the car analogy, the same thing.

659
00:53:39,520 --> 00:53:48,480
[SPEAKER_00] It's you've created a little engine and extracting the fuel from the engine is why these things grow, which is actually different than the car analogy where you bring the fuel in from somewhere else.

660
00:53:48,640 --> 00:53:51,120
[SPEAKER_00] It's actually a perpetual motion machine more so.

661
00:53:51,760 --> 00:53:59,040
[SPEAKER_00] The point you make about, and it's maybe a little handy if you're having that conversation with yourself about like, are we capturing too much value?

662
00:53:59,280 --> 00:54:00,480
[SPEAKER_00] Are we like off of our mission?

663
00:54:01,120 --> 00:54:07,280
[SPEAKER_00] But if you like, actually are investing that capture back into the product, it's not right.

664
00:54:07,520 --> 00:54:10,080
[SPEAKER_00] Like, and I guess that depends on what you do with the profits of a business.

665
00:54:10,480 --> 00:54:12,800
[SPEAKER_00] It just goes into like the fundamentals of capitalism, right?

666
00:54:13,040 --> 00:54:14,240
[SPEAKER_00] Are you reinvesting that in growth?

667
00:54:14,400 --> 00:54:15,920
[SPEAKER_00] Are you reinvesting that in product?

668
00:54:16,640 --> 00:54:17,920
[SPEAKER_00] Are you reinvesting that in both?

669
00:54:18,480 --> 00:54:27,520
[SPEAKER_00] You're not only leaving a value surplus and depending on how much you capture, but you're also leveraging that investment into, hopefully, even more value creation for the customer.

670
00:54:28,160 --> 00:54:31,040
[SPEAKER_02] Lots of hands getting waved and like the accounting on that, right?

671
00:54:31,360 --> 00:54:32,160
[SPEAKER_02] Like it's really hard.

672
00:54:32,560 --> 00:54:41,440
[SPEAKER_02] Well, and I think there are exogenous forces that dictate in most cases., at least with consumer subscription apps, that a lot of the value will be reinvested into the business.

673
00:54:41,840 --> 00:54:42,480
[SPEAKER_02] For two reasons.

674
00:54:43,120 --> 00:54:50,000
[SPEAKER_02] One is because the companies that make it far enough to where they're really honing their value capture engine that have grown to your point earlier, right?

675
00:54:50,240 --> 00:54:55,360
[SPEAKER_02] You probably haven't hired a large growth team until you've gotten to the point where you have a really compelling product and a large user base.

676
00:54:55,680 --> 00:54:58,640
[SPEAKER_02] So the companies that have made it this far have made it this far for a reason.

677
00:54:58,720 --> 00:55:02,640
[SPEAKER_02] And they have a compelling product and they're not mercenaries, or they probably wouldn't have made it this far.

678
00:55:03,200 --> 00:55:12,640
[SPEAKER_02] The second and probably more important reason is the competitive forces around consumer subscription, unless you have a really, really strong network effect, which most consumer subscription apps don't.

679
00:55:13,280 --> 00:55:21,120
[SPEAKER_02] You can't afford not reinvest a lot of that value into the product, because if you don't, someone will come along and they will, and eventually they'll leapfrogue you.

680
00:55:21,760 --> 00:55:36,320
[SPEAKER_01] You talked about a freemium strategy as the paid users subsidizing the value that you're giving away for free to the free users, but you can and do actually capture some amount of value from the free users.

681
00:55:36,640 --> 00:55:45,360
[SPEAKER_01] And if you're using a freemium model, you should be thinking about what value you're capturing from those free users.

682
00:55:45,600 --> 00:55:47,920
[SPEAKER_01] And a good example is all trails, which we've talked about a lot.

683
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:56,880
[SPEAKER_01] But part of the reason their freemium strategy works is that those free users are helping update the data and adding new trails.

684
00:55:57,280 --> 00:56:00,960
[SPEAKER_01] And it's like they're actually contributing value back into the loop.

685
00:56:01,280 --> 00:56:08,240
[SPEAKER_01] And all trails has, I think they have some minimal ads and some sponsorships and things like that, and they have merchandise and they have other things.

686
00:56:08,800 --> 00:56:17,040
[SPEAKER_01] And so when thinking about value capture, we're mostly going to talk about value capture in relation to capture through subscription.

687
00:56:17,440 --> 00:56:24,800
[SPEAKER_01] But when you have a freemium model, you should still also be thinking about what value those free users are adding to the equation.

688
00:56:25,200 --> 00:56:30,880
[SPEAKER_01] So before we move on to the subscription value capture, what are your thoughts on that value capture from the free users?

689
00:56:31,520 --> 00:56:32,480
[SPEAKER_02] I think that's spot on.

690
00:56:32,800 --> 00:56:40,160
[SPEAKER_02] And as with so many things, you need to tailor your strategy around the fundamental constraints of your business.

691
00:56:40,480 --> 00:56:45,920
[SPEAKER_02] So in this case, your growth loops that drive your business will dictate things like what is our trial strategy?

692
00:56:46,480 --> 00:56:49,440
[SPEAKER_02] How aggressive or conservative are we going to be with our paywall placement?

693
00:56:49,840 --> 00:56:59,680
[SPEAKER_02] And for a product like AllTrails or Quizlet for that matter, where so much of the growth is being driven organically, you are getting a ton of value out of those free users.

694
00:57:00,240 --> 00:57:08,880
[SPEAKER_02] In fact, I would argue you're getting value that the business would struggle to exist without, or at least it would be much less valuable, right?

695
00:57:09,120 --> 00:57:11,840
[SPEAKER_02] Because they are the engine that is driving compounding growth.

696
00:57:12,240 --> 00:57:40,800
[SPEAKER_02] On the other end of that spectrum, though, there are cases where you have a product that free users might hang around and dabble, and they might write into customer support every now and then, and they're not adding zero value, but the amount of value they're adding is so incommensurate with the amount of time and maintenance and energy that a company can be spending to support them that it's not always a good thing, that it's not always the case that you want to cater to that free user segment.

697
00:57:41,120 --> 00:57:42,960
[SPEAKER_02] It just really depends on your product and your business model.

698
00:57:43,600 --> 00:57:52,000
[SPEAKER_01] And to your point with Quizlet, I mean, it's perfectly acceptable as a business to say part of our mission as a company is to deliver excess value.

699
00:57:52,080 --> 00:57:57,600
[SPEAKER_01] And part of how we're going to do that is to give way more away to these free users and let the paid users subsidize it.

700
00:57:58,240 --> 00:58:00,880
[SPEAKER_01] But you need to think about, is that what we're doing?

701
00:58:01,200 --> 00:58:03,680
[SPEAKER_01] Or do we need to capture more value from the free users?

702
00:58:04,160 --> 00:58:06,400
[SPEAKER_01] Or how do we capture more value from the free users?

703
00:58:06,560 --> 00:58:10,640
[SPEAKER_01] So I think it's kind of an interesting thought exercise on the freemium side of the equation.

704
00:58:11,040 --> 00:58:18,480
[SPEAKER_02] I think that part of the reason why Quizlet is now one of the most valuable ed tech companies in the world is because they've given away so much value for so long.

705
00:58:18,720 --> 00:58:20,640
[SPEAKER_02] In their case, it really does make sense for their model.

706
00:58:21,120 --> 00:58:31,760
[SPEAKER_02] It's built this really compelling community, not just among students, but there's this cross side network effect between teachers and students that creates this really virtuous cycle, improves retention, improves power word of mouth.

707
00:58:32,320 --> 00:58:39,280
[SPEAKER_02] And it's because Quizlet had no subscription for the first many years of its existence, didn't even have ads for a long time.

708
00:58:39,840 --> 00:58:48,880
[SPEAKER_02] And even after it introduced a subscription, kept its prices quite low, that allowed the company to build this really strong mode and this brand around we're going to do good.

709
00:58:49,200 --> 00:58:50,880
[SPEAKER_02] We're going to put our students and teachers first.

710
00:58:51,360 --> 00:59:00,160
[SPEAKER_02] And there are a lot of companies that tried to take the shortcut and monetize more rapidly that ultimately ended failing because Quizlet just had such an advantage when it came to acquisition.

711
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:09,280
[SPEAKER_01] So let's move on to the five Ps of value capture, paywall, pricing and packaging, payments and promotions.

712
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:13,280
[SPEAKER_01] So it's four categories, five Ps.

713
00:59:14,720 --> 00:59:16,560
[SPEAKER_02] I bundled pricing and packaging together.

714
00:59:17,200 --> 00:59:17,680
[SPEAKER_01] It makes sense.

715
00:59:17,840 --> 00:59:18,320
[SPEAKER_02] It makes sense.

716
00:59:18,480 --> 00:59:19,440
[SPEAKER_02] It's a classic pair.

717
00:59:20,000 --> 00:59:21,200
[SPEAKER_01] So let's talk about paywall.

718
00:59:21,520 --> 00:59:24,720
[SPEAKER_01] In the context of the value capture, how should you be thinking?

719
00:59:25,200 --> 00:59:25,920
[SPEAKER_01] about paywalls?

720
00:59:26,240 --> 00:59:27,840
[SPEAKER_02] I'll break this into two parts.

721
00:59:28,000 --> 00:59:35,120
[SPEAKER_02] There's standard stuff that you can and should do with your paywall that kind of applies to about any consumer or prosumer subscription app.

722
00:59:35,520 --> 00:59:38,640
[SPEAKER_02] And I know you've had Jake Moore from Superwall on the podcast a couple of times.

723
00:59:38,720 --> 00:59:39,920
[SPEAKER_02] I think he's talked through a lot of these.

724
00:59:40,080 --> 00:59:46,800
[SPEAKER_02] So things like when you're just getting started, err on the side of being aggressive with your paywall because one, it will help you start to make revenue.

725
00:59:47,040 --> 00:59:52,640
[SPEAKER_02] But two, it will really filter out the users who are your early adopters, who value your premium product the most.

726
00:59:53,120 --> 00:59:54,240
[SPEAKER_02] So that makes a lot of sense.

727
00:59:54,560 --> 00:59:56,720
[SPEAKER_02] And then there's tactics around use visuals.

728
00:59:56,720 --> 00:59:58,480
[SPEAKER_02] A picture is worth a thousand words.

729
00:59:58,720 --> 00:59:59,840
[SPEAKER_02] A video is better than an image.

730
01:00:00,320 --> 01:00:06,800
[SPEAKER_02] Like, make your paywall as compelling as possible, keep your feature list short, lead with an emotional appeal.

731
01:00:07,360 --> 01:00:10,960
[SPEAKER_02] A lot of this just goes back to front load delight, make things as easy as possible for the user.

732
01:00:11,040 --> 01:00:13,520
[SPEAKER_02] Like, a lot of users don't even read all the text on the paywall, right?

733
01:00:13,680 --> 01:00:16,240
[SPEAKER_02] And so it has to be quick and easy to consume.

734
01:00:16,640 --> 01:00:31,920
[SPEAKER_02] I think where things get really interesting is when you start to move one layer deeper than the kind of general best practices around paywall and get into the specifics around what makes sense for your business, depending on factors like the nature of your product.

735
01:00:32,400 --> 01:00:43,120
[SPEAKER_02] So if you have a more complex product, you're likely going to need to give a user a little bit more time to experience it before you ask them to put their credit card down.

736
01:00:43,600 --> 01:00:44,480
[SPEAKER_02] Pricing similar.

737
01:00:44,640 --> 01:00:47,520
[SPEAKER_02] Like if you have a really high price, you're going to need to be more conservative.

738
01:00:47,600 --> 01:00:49,520
[SPEAKER_02] If you have a lower price, you can probably be more aggressive.

739
01:00:49,840 --> 01:00:51,200
[SPEAKER_02] Growth loops, we already covered.

740
01:00:51,440 --> 01:00:59,680
[SPEAKER_02] So if user generated content or by word of mouth are core to your growth strategy, probably want to have a freemium app experience and create a great experience for free users.

741
01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:06,000
[SPEAKER_02] But if you're a pay to play subscription where it's all about just channeling revenue back into paid ads, then you can be more aggressive.

742
01:01:06,560 --> 01:01:08,000
[SPEAKER_02] And then there are a few other factors.

743
01:01:08,160 --> 01:01:15,520
[SPEAKER_02] Like so target users, adults and prosumers are probably going to be a little bit more patient and have a little bit more money to spend versus students or teenagers.

744
01:01:16,000 --> 01:01:17,120
[SPEAKER_02] So you have to keep that in mind.

745
01:01:17,600 --> 01:01:18,800
[SPEAKER_02] Competition is an obvious one.

746
01:01:19,040 --> 01:01:24,480
[SPEAKER_02] So like the more viable substitutes there are for your product, the more you probably will need to be generous with your paywall.

747
01:01:24,720 --> 01:01:26,400
[SPEAKER_02] And then the last one is trial strategy.

748
01:01:26,960 --> 01:01:34,240
[SPEAKER_02] So where you place the paywall, how aggressively you place the paywall, needs to be compatible with how you've structured your free trial.

749
01:01:34,800 --> 01:01:40,960
[SPEAKER_02] If you're giving away a month free, then paywall away because it's a very soft paywall, right?

750
01:01:41,120 --> 01:01:43,920
[SPEAKER_02] People are still going to get a lot of time to experience the core value prop.

751
01:01:44,320 --> 01:01:50,160
[SPEAKER_02] But if it's a three day free trial or if there's no trial at all, then you probably want to be a little more thoughtful about where you put that experience.

752
01:01:50,560 --> 01:01:55,840
[SPEAKER_01] What are some good examples that you've seen of effective paywall strategies?

753
01:01:56,480 --> 01:02:02,320
[SPEAKER_02] I'll give a couple of outside in examples that I've just observed companies doing what I would consider to be a really good job with this.

754
01:02:02,480 --> 01:02:09,520
[SPEAKER_02] And then I have one example of a company that I'm working directly with now that I mentioned before, Matter, that actually saw pretty significant improvements from some of these changes.

755
01:02:10,000 --> 01:02:14,480
[SPEAKER_02] To start with, Calm, I think is a company that gets referenced a lot in terms of their paywall design.

756
01:02:14,960 --> 01:02:15,840
[SPEAKER_02] There's the obvious stuff.

757
01:02:16,000 --> 01:02:17,280
[SPEAKER_02] They lead with an emotional appeal.

758
01:02:17,520 --> 01:02:21,360
[SPEAKER_02] They have this nice calming imagery of people resting and meditating.

759
01:02:21,920 --> 01:02:23,360
[SPEAKER_02] Their feature list is short and pithy.

760
01:02:23,360 --> 01:02:25,360
[SPEAKER_02] It doesn't take a lot of time to read read or digest.

761
01:02:25,920 --> 01:02:31,280
[SPEAKER_02] They use the standard Amazon yellow best deal recommended tags to highlight the annual plan.

762
01:02:31,840 --> 01:02:35,600
[SPEAKER_02] They break down the effective monthly rate of the annual plan versus the monthly plan.

763
01:02:35,760 --> 01:02:38,400
[SPEAKER_02] They're all the like blocking and tackling stuff that they do really well.

764
01:02:38,880 --> 01:02:50,720
[SPEAKER_02] I think the more subtle thing that is easy to miss with Colm's paywall is one, it's very much built for mobile because most of Colm's consumers are purchasing on mobile and using the app on mobile as opposed to desktop.

765
01:02:50,800 --> 01:02:53,920
[SPEAKER_02] They do have a desktop paywall, but it's, I would guess, it's not what most people are going to.

766
01:02:54,640 --> 01:03:00,560
[SPEAKER_02] And then the second piece is the secret to Colm's success in leapfrogging headspace in many ways was.

767
01:03:00,720 --> 01:03:08,000
[SPEAKER_02] was stumbling over this idea of sleep stories and really solving the most acute pain point people have in this mindfulness space, which is sleeping better.

768
01:03:08,560 --> 01:03:14,720
[SPEAKER_02] And so if you look at their paywall, imagine it's midnight or one in the morning and you can't sleep.

769
01:03:14,880 --> 01:03:15,920
[SPEAKER_02] You've got racing thoughts.

770
01:03:16,240 --> 01:03:20,080
[SPEAKER_02] You open your calm app, maybe you heard about it from a friend or you've been considering paying for it.

771
01:03:20,640 --> 01:03:23,440
[SPEAKER_02] And it's like this nice, soft blue palette.

772
01:03:23,680 --> 01:03:25,440
[SPEAKER_02] It uses a lot of calming language.

773
01:03:26,000 --> 01:03:32,880
[SPEAKER_02] It's just very tailored for their customer and the mind space that their customer is going to be in when they're getting to that purchase decision.

774
01:03:33,360 --> 01:03:39,920
[SPEAKER_02] And so I think that's an example of going beyond the generic advice, going beyond the frameworks and actually applying judgment around what's going to work for their consumer.

775
01:03:40,320 --> 01:03:48,320
[SPEAKER_01] I notice Calm has also experimented a lot now and is leaning in toward customizing the paywall experience for different entry points.

776
01:03:48,960 --> 01:04:02,080
[SPEAKER_01] So like if you enter, I was playing with the app recently and I'm not currently a subscriber, so when you enter from like whatever famous person, Matthew McConaughey reads me to sleep, I think they have like sleep stories from him.

777
01:04:02,720 --> 01:04:07,360
[SPEAKER_01] It's like you tap on that button and then it follows through with Matthew McConaughey.

778
01:04:07,760 --> 01:04:14,720
[SPEAKER_01] But if you hit the paywall without that context, you're not seeing celebrities., you're seeing different sets of value prop.

779
01:04:15,040 --> 01:04:18,080
[SPEAKER_01] And so, you know, and it's a big team and they're able to do this.

780
01:04:18,560 --> 01:04:22,560
[SPEAKER_01] It's harder as a small app to figure out every different entry point and all that.

781
01:04:22,880 --> 01:04:32,640
[SPEAKER_01] But over time, figuring out those entry points and even potentially customizing the paywall to the entry points can be a very effective way to do it, and I think Calm is really killing it on that front.

782
01:04:33,200 --> 01:04:44,720
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, I think that's something where you've seen a lot more innovation recently, is personalizing the paywall or customizing it based on the channel the user came in through or even based on in-app signals that you've got about the user, depending on where the paywall is placed.

783
01:04:45,040 --> 01:04:48,400
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, any other examples top of mind on great paywall strategies?

784
01:04:49,040 --> 01:04:52,320
[SPEAKER_02] I think another example that a lot of people point to is Duolingo.

785
01:04:52,400 --> 01:04:58,240
[SPEAKER_02] I talked about their immersive onboarding experience and getting the user to the point where they're really excited about the product before they get to the paywall.

786
01:04:58,800 --> 01:05:08,080
[SPEAKER_02] Above and beyond all of the standard advice, I think one thing Duolingo really differentiates itself on is this personification of the app through Duo, their owl mascot.

787
01:05:08,640 --> 01:05:13,920
[SPEAKER_02] And when you convert into a trial with Duolingo, which is now Super Duolingo is what they're calling.

788
01:05:14,000 --> 01:05:16,160
[SPEAKER_02] It used to be Duolingo Plus, now it's Super Duolingo.

789
01:05:16,320 --> 01:05:17,840
[SPEAKER_02] Duo turns into an astronaut.

790
01:05:18,160 --> 01:05:21,600
[SPEAKER_02] The whole Chrome of the app experience changes the color, the look, the feel.

791
01:05:21,840 --> 01:05:22,560
[SPEAKER_02] There's more animation.

792
01:05:23,040 --> 01:05:38,400
[SPEAKER_02] That's a missed opportunity that I think a lot of you obviously you're not going to do an optimization like this as a smaller company, but when you get to the point where there's a lot of leverage in optimizing trial start and trial conversion, doing something like that that really celebrates that purchase moment, I think can be really powerful.

793
01:05:38,720 --> 01:05:44,480
[SPEAKER_02] And then the last example I was going to give is this company that I've been working with, Matter that I mentioned previously, the reading app.

794
01:05:45,040 --> 01:05:53,360
[SPEAKER_02] So we followed a lot of the standard advice that Jake provided in terms of just making the paywall more prominent, looking at paywall view rate.

795
01:05:53,600 --> 01:06:00,720
[SPEAKER_02] So like what percentage of all users who install the app are actually viewing the paywall at least once in their first session, in their first week, in their first month?

796
01:06:01,520 --> 01:06:11,120
[SPEAKER_02] And by being a little bit more aggressive about where we place the paywall, but then also by optimizing copy visuals and a number of other things, matter ended up more than doubling the paywall view rate.

797
01:06:11,520 --> 01:06:16,320
[SPEAKER_02] And then that translated into about a thirty percent increase in subscriber conversion.

798
01:06:16,640 --> 01:06:22,400
[SPEAKER_02] And so these things like a lot of them seem small, but in the aggregate, they can actually lead to some pretty significant improvements.

799
01:06:22,880 --> 01:06:27,040
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, thirty percent and doubling the paywall view rate, which we hear that a lot.

800
01:06:27,200 --> 01:06:42,800
[SPEAKER_01] We've talked to a lot of people in the podcasts, like people who didn't have the paywall and onboarding, they moved it to onboarding, which like for them, sometimes it's like a hundred X because the paywall was so deeply buried in the app experience that they're showing the paywall way more than double.

801
01:06:43,200 --> 01:06:52,320
[SPEAKER_01] And then it's like five Xing revenue, not even just a thirty percent increase, but if you're already fairly optimized, there's still opportunities to increase by thirty percent, which is crazy.

802
01:06:52,640 --> 01:06:56,160
[SPEAKER_00] I remember doing optimization stuff around Elevate in the back of the day.

803
01:06:56,400 --> 01:07:00,000
[SPEAKER_00] And like everything is just how close it is to the starting point.

804
01:07:00,320 --> 01:07:01,040
[SPEAKER_00] There's things you can do.

805
01:07:01,200 --> 01:07:06,080
[SPEAKER_00] You can shovel things around, but there's kind of an exponential decay curve in like who sees what in an app.

806
01:07:06,640 --> 01:07:08,560
[SPEAKER_00] And then also you have your home screen in the app.

807
01:07:08,880 --> 01:07:13,360
[SPEAKER_00] That's where ninety percent of the eyeball minutes are going to be, ninety five percent of the eyeballeball minutes.

808
01:07:13,680 --> 01:07:14,880
[SPEAKER_00] That's not even a paywall hack.

809
01:07:14,960 --> 01:07:19,680
[SPEAKER_00] It's a good strategy, but it's just like, make sure every and this is something I learned from a Zynga person.

810
01:07:19,920 --> 01:07:25,040
[SPEAKER_00] Zynga people are really good at monetization, but like, the user should always be one click away from buying.

811
01:07:25,280 --> 01:07:26,640
[SPEAKER_00] You know, you design a store the same way.

812
01:07:26,800 --> 01:07:30,640
[SPEAKER_00] Like, you don't go into a store and there's like massive parts of the store where you can't buy things, right?

813
01:07:30,960 --> 01:07:32,800
[SPEAKER_00] Like, the whole purpose of it is to buy things.

814
01:07:32,880 --> 01:07:34,560
[SPEAKER_00] And you have to think about your app in the same way.

815
01:07:34,800 --> 01:07:36,960
[SPEAKER_00] If you're monetizing, you don't have to be gauche about it.

816
01:07:37,040 --> 01:07:42,640
[SPEAKER_00] Like, you can do it tastefully, but you should always be subtly reminding your customers, like, hey, and that's why I like the Dualingo example.

817
01:07:42,800 --> 01:07:50,400
[SPEAKER_00] I didn't know they changed the Chrome, because like, I think that's a really easy way to remind users you don't have Pro, just so you know., you're not paying, right?

818
01:07:50,880 --> 01:07:52,560
[SPEAKER_00] Even if they don't get any features, right?

819
01:07:52,800 --> 01:07:54,480
[SPEAKER_00] It's just kind of a subtle psychology thing.

820
01:07:54,800 --> 01:07:57,040
[SPEAKER_02] We still love you, but you're not quite as special as you could be.

821
01:07:57,600 --> 01:07:57,680
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

822
01:07:58,000 --> 01:08:02,880
[SPEAKER_00] I mean, I think you can make the argument that a lot of subscription apps in some degree are a luxury product.

823
01:08:03,360 --> 01:08:07,520
[SPEAKER_00] You don't need Duolingo to survive, certainly don't need Duolingo Pro to survive, right?

824
01:08:07,760 --> 01:08:08,800
[SPEAKER_00] You don't need those things.

825
01:08:09,120 --> 01:08:17,040
[SPEAKER_00] So you can take some pages out of what the automotive industry does and like the luxury goods industry and think about it in some ways as a display of status, right?

826
01:08:17,440 --> 01:08:19,360
[SPEAKER_00] I guess it's more about what's behind the paywall.

827
01:08:19,680 --> 01:08:25,600
[SPEAKER_00] But as you're leading someone through that paywall as well, you're selling them on what life will be like on the other side for them, you know what I mean?

828
01:08:25,840 --> 01:08:28,800
[SPEAKER_00] Like, oh, come on, you've got, oh, sir, eight dollars a month.

829
01:08:28,960 --> 01:08:29,840
[SPEAKER_00] Wow, big spender.

830
01:08:30,000 --> 01:08:30,640
[SPEAKER_00] Come on in, right?

831
01:08:30,880 --> 01:08:44,960
[SPEAKER_02] Like, well, and to that point, I've found this isn't always the right answer, but in many cases, there's this one, two punch of trigger a paywall in a user's first session, whether that's immediately after sign up or at the end of an onboarding flow, depends on the product.

832
01:08:45,280 --> 01:08:53,760
[SPEAKER_02] But make sure that a user sees the paywall in their first session, because you'd be shocked how many users of consumer subscription apps don't even know there is a premium version of the product.

833
01:08:54,480 --> 01:09:01,680
[SPEAKER_02] But then pair that with a set of more customized paywalls that are tied to your best premium value props and measure them.

834
01:09:01,840 --> 01:09:10,560
[SPEAKER_02] So you have a free number of uses, but once you get to the end of that free usage quota, then you get a customized paywall that's really honed around that feature.

835
01:09:11,280 --> 01:09:32,960
[SPEAKER_02] And I think what's nice about that is you solve the paywall view rate problem that Jake talks about, which is you have to first just make sure people know your premium product exists, but then instead of like tapping them on the shoulder every single time they open the app, which becomes really annoying really quickly and it's going to churn out a lot of your free users, instead like align the ask around purchasing the product with when the user is actually experiencing the value.

836
01:09:33,040 --> 01:09:33,200
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

837
01:09:33,680 --> 01:09:39,840
[SPEAKER_00] And then being thoughtful about where those like limitations to free usage are I think a lot of David, I'm sure you've talked to a lot of indies.

838
01:09:40,000 --> 01:09:44,960
[SPEAKER_00] This is like classic indie mistake is like, oh, I'm going to bury the purchase thing like in the settings, whatever.

839
01:09:45,040 --> 01:09:47,600
[SPEAKER_00] And like, yeah, they actually have a pretty convincing pro offering.

840
01:09:48,000 --> 01:09:50,320
[SPEAKER_00] Some kids it's just like embarrassed to like, pull it forward.

841
01:09:50,560 --> 01:09:57,520
[SPEAKER_00] This is some advice I had from a mentor, but just being like, these people are getting an app for free, they can look at an app, they can look at the price tag.

842
01:09:57,840 --> 01:09:58,400
[SPEAKER_00] They're not going to die.

843
01:09:58,640 --> 01:10:00,560
[SPEAKER_00] We use it like, don't be annoying about it.

844
01:10:00,640 --> 01:10:01,360
[SPEAKER_00] Like, be thoughtful.

845
01:10:01,440 --> 01:10:06,480
[SPEAKER_00] You don't want to degrade your free user experience completely, but it's okay for free users to give something up.

846
01:10:06,720 --> 01:10:10,000
[SPEAKER_00] If that's just a little bit of degradation in user experience, that's okay.

847
01:10:10,400 --> 01:10:12,880
[SPEAKER_02] If you do it right, the paywall moment in itself.

848
01:10:13,760 --> 01:10:15,280
[SPEAKER_02] I think Duolingo is a great example of that.

849
01:10:15,440 --> 01:10:23,600
[SPEAKER_02] Like, even if you decide not to pay, like, oh, Duolingo in an astronaut suit, that's a great guiding principle from, like, product design and business design in general.

850
01:10:23,920 --> 01:10:26,960
[SPEAKER_00] It's like, even bad moments in a business, they're in support.

851
01:10:27,200 --> 01:10:36,400
[SPEAKER_00] Like, something's bad, you know, whatever, you have an incident, like, whatever it is, there's always ways to think about how you can take that moment and turn it into a net positive experience.

852
01:10:36,960 --> 01:10:38,960
[SPEAKER_00] And sometimes it's just little things, right?

853
01:10:39,200 --> 01:10:43,440
[SPEAKER_00] It's like framings and copy and offering to like make it right.

854
01:10:43,680 --> 01:10:45,280
[SPEAKER_00] That applies to monetization as well.

855
01:10:45,520 --> 01:10:47,040
[SPEAKER_00] The walls and like where you hit stuff.

856
01:10:47,200 --> 01:10:47,280
[SPEAKER_00] So.

857
01:10:47,600 --> 01:10:47,680
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

858
01:10:48,160 --> 01:10:54,160
[SPEAKER_01] And I think you have a little bit more flexibility to your point, Phil, about turning out free users by annoying them.

859
01:10:54,400 --> 01:11:01,840
[SPEAKER_01] It's like the more value you're creating for those users, the more leeway you have to demand something of them.

860
01:11:02,080 --> 01:11:08,000
[SPEAKER_01] So like my fitness pal, I'm not currently subscribed, but I still check calories every now and then, I check my macros or whatever.

861
01:11:08,640 --> 01:11:11,360
[SPEAKER_01] Every single time you open the app, they show up paywall.

862
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:16,960
[SPEAKER_01] I'm not as annoyed anymore because it's like I'm getting so much value when I use it.

863
01:11:17,200 --> 01:11:18,160
[SPEAKER_01] It's a fair trade, right?

864
01:11:19,200 --> 01:11:32,800
[SPEAKER_01] I mean, we've talked about it a lot today, but that kind of balance of how much value you're creating and how much value you're cap capturing, that paywall moment, you just have so much more leeway with those free users if you're giving them so much value.

865
01:11:33,360 --> 01:11:38,880
[SPEAKER_01] But moving on from paywall pricing and packaging are the next two P's of value capture.

866
01:11:39,200 --> 01:11:40,480
[SPEAKER_01] Let's talk pricing and packaging.

867
01:11:41,200 --> 01:11:51,040
[SPEAKER_02] When you look at each of the steps in the subscription value loop and then you look at the individual levers within each step, this is one of those, along with onboarding and a couple of others that has the most leverage.

868
01:11:51,600 --> 01:11:52,960
[SPEAKER_02] Getting pricing and packaging right.

869
01:11:53,200 --> 01:11:57,680
[SPEAKER_02] I think there have been a few episodes you all have had guests on that said, well pricing in the early days doesn't really matter.

870
01:11:57,840 --> 01:11:58,400
[SPEAKER_02] And I agree with that.

871
01:11:58,480 --> 01:12:00,160
[SPEAKER_02] Like in the early days, just find product market fit.

872
01:12:00,640 --> 01:12:08,800
[SPEAKER_02] But once you get to the world I've spent most of my career in, Series B, Series C, trying to get to the point where you can go public, pricing really, really matters.

873
01:12:09,120 --> 01:12:10,560
[SPEAKER_02] And it's really hard to do well.

874
01:12:10,960 --> 01:12:29,280
[SPEAKER_02] And so one of the things that I try to help clients with in my advising business is using some pretty standard tools out there like Van Westendorp and Conjoint Analysis to really get to at least a more accurate view of what the optimal price is for your product.

875
01:12:30,000 --> 01:12:37,360
[SPEAKER_02] So to just briefly break those two down, Van Westendorp Analysis, I actually was listening to the latest podcast episode on my of my run this morning.

876
01:12:37,920 --> 01:12:42,720
[SPEAKER_02] And so I know the CEO of Ladder mentioned Van Westendorp last week, so I won't go into too much detail.

877
01:12:42,800 --> 01:12:46,240
[SPEAKER_02] But the idea is basically you can't ask a user directly, how much would you pay for this product?

878
01:12:46,560 --> 01:12:56,240
[SPEAKER_02] Because research has shown they're going to generally give you a lower price than is actually what they would be willing to pay because they know that any answer they give you could impact what you end up charging.

879
01:12:56,560 --> 01:12:57,840
[SPEAKER_02] So instead you ask a range.

880
01:12:58,000 --> 01:13:02,160
[SPEAKER_02] You ask, at what price would this product feel like it was really expensive?

881
01:13:02,400 --> 01:13:05,040
[SPEAKER_02] At what price would it feel like it's starting to become more of a bargain?

882
01:13:05,200 --> 01:13:07,760
[SPEAKER_02] At what price would it feel cheap, like a really good deal?

883
01:13:07,840 --> 01:13:09,600
[SPEAKER_02] And at what price would it feel too cheap?

884
01:13:10,000 --> 01:13:11,840
[SPEAKER_02] That means you would actually question the quality of the product.

885
01:13:12,560 --> 01:13:15,840
[SPEAKER_02] And then by doing that, you can generate what's called the Van Wessendorp.

886
01:13:16,000 --> 01:13:17,760
[SPEAKER_02] It's basically a price sensitivity meter.

887
01:13:18,000 --> 01:13:21,280
[SPEAKER_02] And so it shows you these intersection points between those four curves.

888
01:13:21,600 --> 01:13:26,320
[SPEAKER_02] And it gives you an optimal price range and also an optimal price point somewhere within that range.

889
01:13:26,880 --> 01:13:32,080
[SPEAKER_02] This tool is really good when you're just looking at what is the overall willingness to pay for my product in the aggregate.

890
01:13:32,800 --> 01:13:37,920
[SPEAKER_02] Or, as was mentioned in the latter episode, you can cut it by segments of users.

891
01:13:38,160 --> 01:13:45,600
[SPEAKER_02] So if I want to know for students versus teachers or for college students versus high school students, what willingness to pay looks like, you can do that segmentation.

892
01:13:46,000 --> 01:13:49,520
[SPEAKER_02] It's not as good at pinpointing willingness to pay for individualsal features.

893
01:13:49,760 --> 01:13:53,120
[SPEAKER_02] And so that's where conjoint analysis can be a useful supplementary tool.

894
01:13:53,520 --> 01:13:57,040
[SPEAKER_02] Because what conjoint does is it presents the users with a set of alternatives.

895
01:13:57,200 --> 01:14:00,880
[SPEAKER_02] It could be two packages, it could be three packages, usually it's no more than two to four.

896
01:14:01,440 --> 01:14:03,760
[SPEAKER_02] And each package has a list of attributes.

897
01:14:04,000 --> 01:14:10,800
[SPEAKER_02] So this could be price, it could be number of super likes per month in the case of a dating app like Tender or Bumble.

898
01:14:11,120 --> 01:14:13,280
[SPEAKER_02] So it could be a range, or it could be binary.

899
01:14:13,440 --> 01:14:15,600
[SPEAKER_02] So it could be a feature that you either get or you don't get.

900
01:14:16,160 --> 01:14:28,480
[SPEAKER_02] And then every user is asked to go through anywhere from like five to ten of these conjo, again, there's a formula that will sort of spit out.

901
01:14:28,560 --> 01:14:31,680
[SPEAKER_02] Okay, what is the relative importance of each of these attributes?

902
01:14:31,920 --> 01:14:35,360
[SPEAKER_02] What is the marginal willingness to pay for each level within each attribute?

903
01:14:35,840 --> 01:14:40,320
[SPEAKER_02] So how much more am I willing to pay for five super likes versus ten super likes versus twenty super likes?

904
01:14:40,640 --> 01:14:49,040
[SPEAKER_02] And when you do all that, you can really hone in on not just how much are people willing to pay overall, but what are the premium features that are driving most of the monetization for our business?

905
01:14:49,280 --> 01:14:50,960
[SPEAKER_02] And again, how does that vary by user segment?

906
01:14:51,600 --> 01:14:53,920
[SPEAKER_01] Where does price testing fit into this?

907
01:14:54,240 --> 01:15:00,320
[SPEAKER_01] Is this kind of hypothesis validation starting with Venn Westendorp and conjoint analysis?

908
01:15:00,720 --> 01:15:06,480
[SPEAKER_01] and then start testing whether your hypothesis formed by this analysis is actually correct?

909
01:15:06,880 --> 01:15:07,520
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, that's right.

910
01:15:07,760 --> 01:15:09,920
[SPEAKER_02] And I view them as tools in the same toolkit.

911
01:15:10,160 --> 01:15:14,880
[SPEAKER_02] And I've even had a healthy debate with a couple of my clients around, like, why don't we just do an AB test?

912
01:15:15,120 --> 01:15:16,640
[SPEAKER_02] Like, why do we even need to run a survey?

913
01:15:17,360 --> 01:15:21,120
[SPEAKER_02] And sometimes that's the right answer, like in general the ultimate survey.

914
01:15:21,440 --> 01:15:22,240
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah, exactly.

915
01:15:22,720 --> 01:15:24,320
[SPEAKER_02] I mean, that's the real asset test, right?

916
01:15:24,720 --> 01:15:25,520
[SPEAKER_02] I'll give you an example.

917
01:15:25,840 --> 01:15:31,760
[SPEAKER_02] I work with an ed tech company down in South America called Udox, and they had two tiers.

918
01:15:32,000 --> 01:15:36,880
[SPEAKER_02] So they had Udox Lite and Udox Pro, and then they had monthly and annual subscriptions.

919
01:15:37,280 --> 01:15:41,920
[SPEAKER_02] And one thing I'll say right off the start is when you're an early stage consumer company, ideally you want one plan.

920
01:15:42,560 --> 01:15:54,240
[SPEAKER_02] You want one plan that is either annual only or that is monthly and annual because any more complexity than that and you're going to lose conversion on the margin simply because people don't have the attention span to make a decision.

921
01:15:54,320 --> 01:15:54,960
[SPEAKER_02] That's that complex.

922
01:15:55,120 --> 01:15:57,760
[SPEAKER_02] If they're a prosumer or it's B2B SaaS, different story.

923
01:15:57,840 --> 01:15:59,760
[SPEAKER_02] But for consumer, you want to keep it as simple as possible.

924
01:16:00,080 --> 01:16:01,200
[SPEAKER_02] So we could have run an AB test.

925
01:16:01,360 --> 01:16:07,920
[SPEAKER_02] And if we'd run an AB test, we would have figured out what is the optimal annual and monthly price for the light and pro tiers respectively.

926
01:16:08,160 --> 01:16:12,640
[SPEAKER_02] But what happened is we ran the Van Westendorp and we looked at the light versus pro plans.

927
01:16:12,640 --> 01:16:23,360
[SPEAKER_02] So we compared the Pro plans with the Van Westendorp analysis and we saw that the marginal willingness to pay for the Pro plan was very, very small relative to the Light plan.

928
01:16:24,000 --> 01:16:28,960
[SPEAKER_02] And we also found that the marginal willingness to pay for annual relative to monthly was fairly low.

929
01:16:29,520 --> 01:16:36,720
[SPEAKER_02] And in some cases, this just comes down to, you're talking about college students in South America, they don't have a ton of disposable income in many cases.

930
01:16:37,200 --> 01:16:39,920
[SPEAKER_02] So some of it just comes down to the ability to pay for the product.

931
01:16:40,720 --> 01:17:01,440
[SPEAKER_02] But then we did the conjoint and in the conjoint, we actually found that student interest in ebook content generated by publishers that was like the biggest incremental value prop for the Pro plan was you don't just get access to user generated content, you get professional publisher content was actually in many cases the lowest marginal to pay premium feature.

932
01:17:02,080 --> 01:17:15,120
[SPEAKER_02] And so as a result, what the company ended up doing was streamlining down to a single tier, just getting rid of the Pro tier and only having what they now call Udocs plus, and then lowering their annual price.

933
01:17:15,760 --> 01:17:18,320
[SPEAKER_02] And so they had a much simpler set of plans.

934
01:17:19,040 --> 01:17:23,920
[SPEAKER_02] They had a lower annual price that was more aligned with willingness to pay of their target user base.

935
01:17:24,560 --> 01:17:36,160
[SPEAKER_02] And then also importantly, because they got rid of the ebook content, which had a pretty significant cost component to it because they had licensing deals with these publishers, they actually improved their margins and as a result their LTV-CAC ratio.

936
01:17:36,480 --> 01:17:47,440
[SPEAKER_02] And so in aggregate, and I've talked to the founder about sharing this information in aggregate, this ended up driving a twelve percent increase in subscriber conversion and a ten percent increase in net subscription revenue.

937
01:17:48,000 --> 01:17:52,720
[SPEAKER_02] So this is a case where AB testing is fast and it's effective, but it's kind of a blunt instrument.

938
01:17:53,440 --> 01:17:57,040
[SPEAKER_02] And surveys can give you some deeper insights when you do them the right way.

939
01:17:57,360 --> 01:17:57,360
[UNKNOWN] Yeah.

940
01:17:57,920 --> 01:18:00,640
[SPEAKER_01] And then doing the Van Wessendorp and the Conjoint.

941
01:18:01,040 --> 01:18:10,160
[SPEAKER_01] analysis helps you create a thesis for why the prices you're going to test should be tested versus just throwing crap against the wall.

942
01:18:10,640 --> 01:18:11,760
[SPEAKER_02] That's my favorite method.

943
01:18:12,800 --> 01:18:13,120
[SPEAKER_02] It works.

944
01:18:13,600 --> 01:18:14,480
[SPEAKER_02] It works until it doesn't.

945
01:18:14,800 --> 01:18:18,640
[SPEAKER_02] Hold your finger in the wind, be like, 4.99.

946
01:18:19,600 --> 01:18:21,440
[SPEAKER_02] That's the right answer for the early days.

947
01:18:22,240 --> 01:18:22,320
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah.

948
01:18:23,120 --> 01:18:25,360
[SPEAKER_01] So the next P is payments.

949
01:18:25,920 --> 01:18:28,080
[SPEAKER_01] What do you mean by payments in this context?

950
01:18:28,720 --> 01:18:28,800
[SPEAKER_02] Yeah.

951
01:18:29,040 --> 01:18:31,040
[SPEAKER_02] So payments, I break down into a few things.

952
01:18:31,440 --> 01:18:45,680
[SPEAKER_02] One is I think a lot of people don't realize the degree to which alternative payment methods or APMs are surpassing in many cases credit cards, the biggest ones being obviously Visa, MasterCard, American Express as the primary forum of e commerce, right?

953
01:18:45,920 --> 01:18:49,360
[SPEAKER_02] Like that's happening in a lot of international markets and it's even starting to happen in the US.

954
01:18:50,080 --> 01:18:56,240
[SPEAKER_02] And so one is just making sure you're aware of the alternative payment methods that really matter in your geography and for your user base.

955
01:18:56,960 --> 01:19:04,400
[SPEAKER_02] The second is your purchase flow and just making sure that you're being as clear and transparent as possible with users around how your trial works.

956
01:19:04,800 --> 01:19:12,480
[SPEAKER_02] And so the canonical example here is the Blinkist ethical subscription design pattern, which I think JC Day was on your podcast and talked about how effective that was.

957
01:19:12,480 --> 01:19:14,800
[SPEAKER_02] That was Quizlet ended up adopting that pattern.

958
01:19:14,960 --> 01:19:16,560
[SPEAKER_02] Many other consumer apps have as well.

959
01:19:17,040 --> 01:19:24,080
[SPEAKER_02] It's one of those rare win-win where it drives a huge amount of incremental upside in terms of subscriber conversion revenue, but it's also just the right thing to do.

960
01:19:25,120 --> 01:19:26,720
[SPEAKER_02] It really builds trust with your users.

961
01:19:27,040 --> 01:19:28,800
[SPEAKER_02] And then the last one is cancellation flows.

962
01:19:28,960 --> 01:19:30,960
[SPEAKER_02] And this is one that I think is tricky, right?

963
01:19:31,280 --> 01:19:37,920
[SPEAKER_02] Because you want to remind the user of what they're losing if they give up the premium plan, but you don't want to be a jerk, right?

964
01:19:38,160 --> 01:19:44,720
[SPEAKER_02] You don't want to adopt these black hat patterns that many companies end up doing in some cases just because they see all the other companies doing it.

965
01:19:44,960 --> 01:19:57,040
[SPEAKER_02] And so you need to make sure sure that you're not preventing users from canceling the subscription if that's what they want to do, but it's okay to remind them along the way, like, hey, why are you leaving?

966
01:19:57,200 --> 01:19:57,920
[SPEAKER_02] Can we fix it?

967
01:19:58,080 --> 01:19:58,720
[SPEAKER_02] Is the price too high?

968
01:19:58,960 --> 01:20:00,080
[SPEAKER_02] Okay, we can offer you a discount.

969
01:20:00,320 --> 01:20:01,040
[SPEAKER_02] Is it something else?

970
01:20:01,200 --> 01:20:02,880
[SPEAKER_02] Like, is there anything we can do to ameliorate this?

971
01:20:03,040 --> 01:20:04,640
[SPEAKER_02] And if the answer is no, then fine, they cancel.

972
01:20:04,960 --> 01:20:20,160
[SPEAKER_02] And I think this is going to become more and more important because there's legislation that is working its way through at both the federal and state level now in the US that is actually going to mandate that more and more subscription apps basically make it as easy to cancel as it was to sign up.

973
01:20:20,320 --> 01:20:26,960
[SPEAKER_02] And so you're not going to be able to get away with some of the stuff that some companies have been doing for a while where you kind of make cancellation really difficult to find.

974
01:20:27,360 --> 01:20:27,440
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah.

975
01:20:27,600 --> 01:20:29,520
[SPEAKER_01] And the app stores make that really easy.

976
01:20:30,160 --> 01:20:46,640
[SPEAKER_01] But I did want to highlight when you were talking about alternative payment methods, I've been hearing this from RevenueCat customers that when they are offering web payments, it's not enough to just offer like credit card and Apple Pay and Apple Pay is kind of the happy easy path for a lot of folks.

977
01:20:47,200 --> 01:20:50,480
[SPEAKER_01] But like pay with Venmo, pay with PayPal, all those.

978
01:20:50,960 --> 01:20:57,040
[SPEAKER_01] It's like if someone's potentially going to give you money, make it as easy as possible for them to give you that money.

